1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,511
Look at this coordinate grid.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:07,807
Every point has a position. Every arrow has
a direction. Everything seems perfectly
ordinary.

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:10,555
But watch what happens when we apply a
matrix.

4
00:00:10,675 --> 00:00:12,760
The entire grid begins to stretch and tilt.

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:17,169
Vectors move, squares become
parallelograms, and space itself appears to
change.

6
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:20,419
What if matrices are not just collections
of numbers?

7
00:00:20,539 --> 00:00:22,542
What if they describe how space transforms?

8
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:25,083
Today, we are going to discover the
geometric meaning

9
00:00:25,203 --> 00:00:29,580
behind one of the most important ideas in
linear algebra: linear transformations.

10
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:31,481
Let's begin with a vector.

11
00:00:31,601 --> 00:00:35,418
A vector can be represented as an arrow
with a magnitude and a direction.

12
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:40,221
When we add two vectors, we place the
second arrow at the tip of the first.

13
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:43,635
When we multiply a vector by a number, we
scale it.

14
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:49,536
Now imagine a machine that takes any vector
as input and produces another vector as
output.

15
00:00:49,656 --> 00:00:51,262
We call this a transformation.

16
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:54,427
But a linear transformation follows two
special rules.

17
00:00:54,547 --> 00:01:00,250
Transforming the sum of two vectors gives
the same result as transforming them
separately and then adding them.

18
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:06,276
Similarly, scaling a vector before
transforming it gives the same result as
scaling it afterward.

19
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:09,990
These properties guarantee that the origin
stays fixed.

20
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:14,773
They also explain why a linear
transformation changes space in such a
structured way.

21
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:17,159
Here is where things become interesting.

22
00:01:17,279 --> 00:01:21,596
Every vector in two-dimensional space can
be constructed using just two special
vectors.

23
00:01:21,716 --> 00:01:24,028
The green vector points one unit to the
right.

24
00:01:24,148 --> 00:01:26,187
The red vector points one unit upward.

25
00:01:26,307 --> 00:01:28,563
These are called the standard basis
vectors.

26
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:30,578
Now consider this matrix.

27
00:01:30,698 --> 00:01:32,614
Its first row contains two and one.

28
00:01:32,734 --> 00:01:34,941
Its second row contains zero and one.

29
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:38,747
The first column tells us where the green
basis vector moves.

30
00:01:38,867 --> 00:01:40,988
It lands at the coordinates two, zero.

31
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:44,785
The second column tells us where the red
basis vector moves.

32
00:01:44,905 --> 00:01:46,436
It lands at one, one.

33
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:51,096
Now watch the entire coordinate grid
transform along with these vectors.

34
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:55,051
Because the transformation is linear,

35
00:01:55,171 --> 00:02:00,262
knowing where these two basis vectors land
is enough to determine where every other
vector goes.

36
00:02:00,382 --> 00:02:04,608
Four numbers can describe the
transformation of an entire two-dimensional
space.

37
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:08,134
Let's understand this using an actual
example.

38
00:02:08,254 --> 00:02:11,066
Consider the yellow vector with coordinates
two, one.

39
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:17,049
Instead of immediately multiplying numbers,
think about how this vector is constructed.

40
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:22,905
We move two units along the green basis
vector, followed by one unit along the red
basis vector.

41
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:29,732
In other words, our vector is two times the
first basis vector, plus the second basis
vector.

42
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,418
Now apply our linear transformation.

43
00:02:32,538 --> 00:02:35,008
The green basis vector moves to two, zero.

44
00:02:35,128 --> 00:02:37,523
The red basis vector moves to one, one.

45
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:42,170
But remember, linear transformations
preserve linear combinations.

46
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:46,694
So our original vector must become two
copies of the transformed green vector,

47
00:02:46,814 --> 00:02:49,506
plus one copy of the transformed red
vector.

48
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:53,243
Two times the vector two, zero gives us
four, zero.

49
00:02:53,363 --> 00:02:56,342
Adding the vector one, one gives us five,
one.

50
00:02:56,462 --> 00:02:57,537
And there it is.

51
00:02:57,657 --> 00:03:01,904
Our original vector, two, one, has
transformed into five, one.

52
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:07,589
Now let's compare this geometric approach
with ordinary matrix multiplication.

53
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:12,472
For the first coordinate, multiply two by
two, then add one times one.

54
00:03:12,592 --> 00:03:13,708
The result is five.

55
00:03:14,150 --> 00:03:18,748
For the second coordinate, multiply zero by
two, then add one times one.

56
00:03:18,868 --> 00:03:19,927
The result is one.

57
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:23,349
Both methods give exactly the same answer.

58
00:03:23,469 --> 00:03:24,964
This is not a coincidence.

59
00:03:25,084 --> 00:03:30,645
Matrix multiplication works because each
matrix column represents a transformed
basis vector.

60
00:03:30,765 --> 00:03:36,015
The original vector's coordinates tell us
how much of each transformed basis vector
to use.

61
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:43,103
For any input coordinates x and y, this
matrix produces two x plus y, and y.

62
00:03:43,223 --> 00:03:48,814
What once looked like a mechanical
calculation is actually a description of
movement through space.

63
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:52,074
Let's return to our coordinate grid.

64
00:03:52,194 --> 00:03:54,615
Consider this square with sides of length
one.

65
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:56,678
Apply the same transformation.

66
00:03:56,798 --> 00:03:58,819
The square stretches into a parallelogram.

67
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:04,081
Notice that the origin has not moved, and
the grid lines remain straight.

68
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:07,896
In this example, parallel grid lines also
remain parallel.

69
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:11,434
Different matrices produce different
geometric effects.

70
00:04:11,554 --> 00:04:13,308
One matrix can rotate space.

71
00:04:13,428 --> 00:04:14,521
Another can reflect it.

72
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:16,959
Another can stretch or compress it.

73
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:19,137
And another can shear it sideways.

74
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:21,833
These transformations appear everywhere.

75
00:04:21,953 --> 00:04:24,505
Computer graphics uses them to manipulate
objects.

76
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:26,681
Robotics uses them to describe motion.

77
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:29,483
Engineering uses them to model changes in
coordinates.

78
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:34,605
Behind each application is the same
fundamental idea: representing how vectors
transform.

79
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:37,212
Let's remember three important ideas.

80
00:04:37,332 --> 00:04:40,156
First, matrices can represent linear
transformations.

81
00:04:40,276 --> 00:04:43,098
Second, their columns tell us where the
basis vectors land.

82
00:04:43,218 --> 00:04:48,233
Third, matrix multiplication combines those
transformed basis vectors using the
original coordinates.

83
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:51,890
The next time you see a matrix, don't just
look at the numbers.

84
00:04:52,010 --> 00:04:53,086
Imagine the grid moving.

85
00:04:53,206 --> 00:04:54,505
Imagine the vectors changing.

86
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:57,382
A matrix is not just a table of numbers.

87
00:04:57,502 --> 00:04:59,515
It is a description of how space changes.
